{"name":"Kornerupine","hex":"#324632","fetched":"2026-10-28","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kornerupine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rare boro-silicate mineral with the chemical formula (Mg,Fe)4(Al,Fe)6(SiO4,BO4)5(O,OH)2, some varieties of which are valued as gemstones."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kornerupine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kornerupine","blocks":[{"text":"Kornerupine (also called Prismatine) is a rare boro-silicate mineral with the chemical formula (Mg,Fe2+)4(Al,Fe3+)6(SiO4,BO4)5(O,OH)2. It crystallizes in the orthorhombic – dipyramidal crystal system as brown, green, yellow to colorless slender tourmaline like prisms or in massive fibrous forms. It has a Mohs hardness of 7 and a specific gravity of 3.3 to 3.34. Its indices of refraction are nα=1.660 – 1.671, nβ=1.673 – 1.683 and nγ=1.674 – 1.684."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Kornerupine-162065.jpg/330px-Kornerupine-162065.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kornerupine","wikidata":"Q419092"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Kornerupine-162065.jpg/330px-Kornerupine-162065.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Kornerupine-162065.jpg/330px-Kornerupine-162065.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKornerupine-162065.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Ardea cinerea (Grey heron) eating fish","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Ardea_cinerea_%28Grey_heron%29_eating_fish.jpg/500px-Ardea_cinerea_%28Grey_heron%29_eating_fish.jpg","ratio":1.3334522111269616,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardea_cinerea_(Grey_heron)_eating_fish.jpg","share":0.284375},{"title":"Grey headed fly